he e7 pawn in place. The e7 pawn is vital because it blocks the White Bishop on b4 from attacking the Black Bishop on f8. If the e-file were opened, White could exchange bishops or prevent Black from castling comfortably. By playing Nf6, Black maintains this shield while developing.\n\nReason: Developing the Knight to f6 is the most active and flexible option. It restricts White's center expansion (e4) and prepares for castling without creating immediate weaknesses or opening diagonals that favor White's pieces.\n\nAnswer: N: g8-f6"}
